// src/pages/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-not-cooling.astro
// Info Wave W5 page 1/9 — "lg refrigerator not cooling" (3,600/mo KD0). Brand sniper.
// LG differentiator: linear-compressor no-cool -- LEGIT class-action settlement (Bentley/
// Sosenko v. LG, D.N.J., final approval Dec 2020, >1.5M consumers, 2014-2017 models, cash
// relief + warranty extension; NO "file a claim" -- window closed) AND separately LG's
// 10-year linear/inverter compressor PARTS warranty (actionable: "check purchase date").
// Settlement vs warranty distinct paragraph. Universal cooling path. Food-safety block
// (USDA/FDA: 4h fridge / 48h freezer / 40F-2h) + "call today". Codes cross-ref lg-error-
// codes (not duplicated). Safe DIY only. umbrella<->sniper links. $89.

import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../../../components/ServiceHero.astro';
import { MAIN_PHONE } from '../../../data/branches';
import { BRANCH_LOCATIONS_GEO_NEUTRAL } from '../../../lib/build-location-array';

const phone = MAIN_PHONE;
const phoneRaw = MAIN_PHONE.replace(/\D/g, '');
const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-not-cooling/";

const title = "LG Refrigerator Not Cooling? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "LG refrigerator not cooling? The 2014–2017 linear compressor is a known issue with a 10-year parts warranty worth checking. Plus the evaporator fan, coils, and how long your food is safe. Same-day help.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my LG refrigerator not cooling?",
    a: "On an LG that runs but won't cool, the causes fall into a short list. The signature one on LG is the linear compressor — LG's 2014 to 2017 models had a documented no-cooling compressor defect (more on the warranty below). Beyond the compressor, the usual causes are a failed evaporator fan (which shows as 'freezer cold, fridge warm'), dirty or blocked condenser coils, a defrost system that's iced over the evaporator, and a failed thermistor or control board. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are yours to do; the compressor and sealed system are a tech job. If your LG stopped cooling, it's a same-day call — food safety is on a clock."
  },
  {
    q: "Is my LG covered by the compressor warranty or the class-action settlement?",
    a: "Two separate things, and it's worth keeping them straight. The class-action settlement (Bentley/Sosenko v. LG, given final approval in December 2020) covered 2014–2017 LG refrigerators with the linear-compressor defect and provided cash relief and a warranty extension — but its claim window closed years ago, so there's no settlement claim to file now. What still helps today is LG's own manufacturer warranty: LG covers the linear/inverter compressor for 10 years on parts. So if your LG was built roughly 2014–2022 and the compressor has failed, that compressor part may be free under the 10-year warranty — you'd still pay diagnosis and labor, but the expensive part is covered. Check your original purchase date and model; our techs verify it before quoting."
  },
  {
    q: "My LG freezer is cold but the fridge is warm — what's wrong?",
    a: "That specific split — a cold freezer with a warm fresh-food section — is the classic evaporator fan failure. On most LG refrigerators the evaporator and its fan live in the freezer, and the fan blows the cold air up into the fridge compartment. When the fan motor fails or frost from a defrost problem jams it, the freezer stays cold but no cold air reaches the fridge. It's a common, fixable fault (a fan motor or the defrost system), and it's a tech job inside the cabinet. Keep the doors closed while you wait — the fridge food is on a roughly four-hour clock."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix an LG refrigerator that's not cooling myself?",
    a: "Some of it, and it's worth trying before the food is at risk. Vacuum the condenser coils (behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate), make sure the vents inside aren't blocked by food, confirm the door seals fully, check the temperature setting, and give the unit a power-cycle (unplug a minute, restart). Pull the fridge a few inches off the wall so it can shed heat, which matters more in an LA summer. What you should not do is open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor or start relay, or replace the control board — those are shock and refrigerant risks for a tech. If it still won't cool, call the same day."
  },
  {
    q: "How long is food safe in an LG that stopped cooling?",
    a: "Per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door kept closed, and a full freezer holds a safe temperature for roughly 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if you keep it shut. Perishable food — meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, leftovers — that's been above 40°F for 2 hours or more should be thrown out. When in doubt, throw it out, and never taste food to check. That 4-hour fridge window is why a no-cool refrigerator is a same-day call, not a next-week one — keep the doors closed and get a tech out before it closes."
  }
];

const schemaJsons = [
  {
