---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/lg-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="LG Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="LG Refrigerator Not Cooling? Start Here — and Check the Compressor Warranty"
  subtitle="LG's 2014–2017 linear compressor is a known no-cool cause with a 10-year parts warranty worth checking. Here's the full cause list, what you can safely do, and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="LG Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A running-but-warm LG is usually the compressor, the fan, or the coils.</h2>

    <p><strong>When an LG refrigerator runs but won't cool, the causes are a short list: the linear compressor (LG's 2014–2017 models had a documented no-cool defect — and there's a 10-year compressor warranty worth checking, below), a failed evaporator fan that shows as "freezer cold, fridge warm," dirty condenser coils, a defrost system iced over, or a bad thermistor or control board. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are safe to do yourself; the compressor and sealed system are a tech job. Either way, treat a no-cool fridge as a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 COMPRESSOR (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The LG linear compressor</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The known issue — and the warranty that still helps.</h2>

    <p>LG's linear compressors in French-door and side-by-side refrigerators built from <strong>2014 to 2017</strong> had a widely documented no-cooling defect. It led to a class-action settlement — <em>Bentley/Sosenko v. LG Electronics USA</em>, given final approval in December 2020, covering more than 1.5 million consumers with cash relief and a warranty extension. Two things to know about that:</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>The settlement's claim window is closed</h3>
      <p>The settlement was finalized years ago and its deadline to file has long passed, so there's no settlement claim to submit now. What it does confirm is that the compressor defect was real and widespread — not something you caused or did wrong.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>LG's 10-year compressor warranty is separate — and still worth checking</h3>
      <p>Distinct from the settlement, LG's manufacturer warranty covers the linear/inverter compressor for <strong>10 years on parts</strong>. So if your LG was built roughly 2014–2022 and the compressor has failed, the compressor part itself may be free under that warranty. You'd still pay diagnosis and labor, but the expensive part can be no-charge. Check your original purchase date and model number — our techs verify the date and confirm it's actually the compressor (not the fan or board) before quoting, so you claim the warranty part when it applies.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why an LG stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't shed the heat the compressor pulls out, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. It's the most common homeowner-fixable cause, and it matters more in an LA summer when the kitchen runs hot.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. A common, fixable fault, and a tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Linear compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool (or won't start), the sealed system is the fault — see the warranty section above, since the part may be covered. Diagnosing it needs gauges and is tech-only.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass, and cooling drops off. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermistor or control board</h3>
      <p>A misreading temperature sensor or a failed main board can stop the fridge from calling for cooling. LG may show a temperature-sensor code — see our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-error-codes/">LG refrigerator error codes</a> guide. Both are tech replacements.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils.</strong> Behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual no-cool cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents.</strong> Make sure food isn't blocking the vents between the freezer and fridge sections.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and setting.</strong> Confirm the doors seal fully and the temperature is set correctly (not bumped warm).</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle.</strong> Pull the fridge a few inches off the wall so it can shed heat, then unplug it for a minute and restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch for improvement over a few hours,</strong> but don't wait past the food-safety window if it's clearly warm inside.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor or start relay, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool LG is a same-day call, not a next-week one. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the vents are clear, and the LG still won't cool, the fault is inside — the evaporator fan, the defrost system, a thermistor or board, or the compressor and sealed system. Our techs test each in order, confirm whether the compressor is covered by LG's 10-year warranty before quoting, and carry the common LG fan motors and defrost parts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide (all brands), and if it's specifically the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Codes: <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-error-codes/">LG refrigerator error codes</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/lg-refrigerator-repair/">LG refrigerator repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/irvine/refrigerator-repair/">Irvine</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>LG refrigerator not cooling? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

